# CSS @supports — Feature Detection in CSS

## 1. What Is @supports?

CSS `@supports` is a feature detection rule that allows developers to check whether a browser supports a particular CSS property or value before applying styles.

Imagine you're using a modern CSS feature such as `backdrop-filter`.

What happens if someone's browser doesn't support it?

Instead of letting the design break, you can provide a fallback style.

This is called **progressive enhancement** — building a working experience first and enhancing it when the browser supports additional features.

## 2. Why Is It Useful?

`@supports` helps developers:

- Build websites that work across different browsers.
- Use modern CSS features safely.
- Provide fallback styles.
- Avoid depending on unsupported CSS properties.
- Gradually introduce newer web technologies.

## 3. Basic Syntax

```css
@supports (property: value) {
    /* Styles applied when the browser supports the feature */
}
```

For example:

```css
@supports (display: grid) {
    .container {
        display: grid;
    }
}
```

The browser checks whether it supports `display: grid`.

If supported, the styles inside the block are applied.

## 4. A Practical Example: Glassmorphism

Imagine creating a transparent card with a blurred background.

The `backdrop-filter` property creates this effect, but you should provide a fallback.

### HTML

```html
<div class="card">
    <h2>Hello, Nikitha!</h2>
    <p>Welcome to my website.</p>
</div>
```

### CSS

```css
.card {
    background: white;
    padding: 30px;
    border-radius: 15px;
}

/* Enhanced styling */
@supports (backdrop-filter: blur(10px)) {
    .card {
        background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
        backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
    }
}
```

The card has a basic white background by default.

If the browser supports `backdrop-filter`, the enhanced glass effect is applied.

## 5. Using `@supports not`

You can also check whether a browser does NOT support a feature.

```css
@supports not (display: grid) {
    .container {
        display: block;
    }
}
```

This applies the fallback when Grid is unsupported.

It is useful when you want to provide alternative layouts for older browsers.

## 6. Combining Multiple Conditions

You can combine feature checks using logical operators.

### Using AND

Both conditions must be supported.

```css
@supports (display: grid) and (gap: 20px) {
    .container {
        display: grid;
        gap: 20px;
    }
}
```

### Using OR

At least one condition must be supported.

```css
@supports (display: grid) or (display: flex) {
    .container {
        display: flex;
    }
}
```

### Using NOT

Checks whether a feature is unsupported.

```css
@supports not (display: grid) {
    .container {
        display: block;
    }
}
```

## 7. Testing CSS Feature Support With JavaScript

CSS feature detection is also possible through JavaScript using `CSS.supports()`.

```javascript
if (CSS.supports("display", "grid")) {
    console.log("CSS Grid is supported!");
} else {
    console.log("CSS Grid is not supported.");
}
```

You can also test a complete CSS condition:

```javascript
console.log(
    CSS.supports("(display: grid) and (gap: 20px)")
);
```

It returns a Boolean value: `true` or `false`.

## 8. @supports vs Media Queries

These two CSS rules solve different problems.

| Feature | @supports | @media |
|---|---|---|
| Checks | Browser feature support | Device or environment conditions |
| Main purpose | Feature detection | Responsive and conditional styling |
| Example | `display: grid` support | Screen width |
| Common use | CSS fallbacks | Mobile layouts |

Example:

```css
@media (max-width: 600px) {
    .container {
        padding: 10px;
    }
}

@supports (display: grid) {
    .container {
        display: grid;
    }
}
```

You can use both together.

## 9. Important Things to Remember

- `@supports` checks CSS feature support, not browser identity.
- A successful feature check does not guarantee identical rendering across browsers.
- Some CSS features have partial or implementation-specific support.
- Always provide sensible default styles outside the feature query.
- Use progressive enhancement rather than making essential content depend on an optional CSS feature.

## 10. Common Mistakes

- Writing fallback styles inside `@supports` instead of outside it.
- Assuming every browser supports every modern CSS feature.
- Using `@supports` to detect screen sizes.
- Assuming support for one CSS property guarantees the entire design will work perfectly.
- Forgetting to test the fallback experience.

## 11. Key Takeaways

- `@supports` is a CSS feature detection rule.
- It checks whether a browser supports a CSS property and value.
- `@supports not` checks for unsupported features.
- `and` and `or` allow multiple conditions.
- JavaScript provides the equivalent `CSS.supports()` method.
- It helps developers build more resilient websites through progressive enhancement.

## One-Line Summary

**CSS @supports allows developers to apply enhanced styles only when a browser supports the required CSS features, while preserving fallback experiences for other browsers.**
